{
 "schema": "path_character_crosscheck/1",
 "note": "Shared fixture. The per-column path character exists twice -- in the offline compiler (Python, path_pieces.column_character) and in the plugin lab (Java, LabGeometry.character) -- for the same reason the height profile does: the compiler authors without a server and the lab classifies at runtime against live blocks. The two had drifted into answering different questions. BRIDGE meant water in Java and a void in Python; LANDING meant the first or last column in Java and a slope reversal in Python; Java had no ENTRANCE at all, and Java tested slope BEFORE water, so a sloping column over a river read as a staircase. One rule now, and both sides run this.",
 "priority": [
  "ENTRANCE",
  "BRIDGE",
  "STAIR",
  "BEND",
  "STRAIGHT"
 ],
 "rules": {
  "entrance": "the first or last column, when termini are declared; a structural fact outranks the ground",
  "bridge": "water OR profile standing span_deviation above raw; both reasons, one piece",
  "stair": "the profile differs from the previous column",
  "bend": "the caller says the line turns here",
  "straight": "what is left",
  "span_deviation": 3,
  "landing": "NOT a column character. It is synthesised between two stair runs that reverse, which is a grouping decision and the compiler owns it alone."
 },
